{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Bkuah6Qiu7fRPiWTJq5Xn8","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Verification Report — Round 3\n     2→\n     3→## Candidate Summary\n     4→- psi = -log(1 + t) - log(1 + x)\n     5→- alpha = 1\n     6→- s = -1\n     7→- lambda = -1/50\n     8→- Other params: none (same ansatz as Round 2, only λ changed from -1/20 to -1/50)\n     9→\n    10→## Condition Results\n    11→\n    12→### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n    13→- Status: **True** (proven)\n    14→- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): **True** (proven)\n    15→- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): **True** (proven)\n    16→- Symbolic expression (1a): `(24c²(1+t)² + 25(1+x)²) / (1250·(1+t)^(99/50)·(1+x)^(99/50))`\n    17→- Symbolic expression (1b): `(25c²(1+t)² + 24(1+x)²) / (1250·(1+t)^(99/50)·(1+x)^(99/50))`\n    18→- Numerical sampling: 1a min = 1.90×10⁻⁴, 1b min = 1.82×10⁻⁴ (both strictly positive)\n    19→- Diagnosis: Both expressions are ratios with positive denominators. The numerators are sums of two manifestly positive terms (since c > 0, t ≥ 0, x ≥ 0, each squared term ≥ 1). Specifically:\n    20→  - 1a numerator: 24c²(1+t)² ≥ 24c² > 0 and 25(1+x)² ≥ 25 > 0\n    21→  - 1b numerator: 25c²(1+t)² ≥ 25c² > 0 and 24(1+x)² ≥ 24 > 0\n    22→\n    23→  This is a clean, rigorous proof. The key property enabling it is that with α = 1, the wave operator □ψ and the energy form ψ̃ coincide (since □ψ = ψ_tt − c²ψ_xx and ψ̃ = (ψ_t)² − c²(ψ_x)², and for this ansatz ψ_tt = (ψ_t)², ψ_xx = (ψ_x)²). This \"self-similar\" property of log functions makes L and L₁ have a particularly simple relationship.\n    24→\n    25→### Necessary Condition 2: lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    26→- Status: **True** (proven by SymPy)\n    27→- λψ = (1/50)·(log(1+t) + log(1+x)) = (1/50)·log((1+t)(1+x))\n    28→- Limit value: +∞\n    29→- Diagnosis: Since λ = −1/50 and ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x), we get λψ = (1/50)·log((1+t)(1+x)). As x → +∞ with t fixed, this diverges to +∞. Trivially proven.\n    30→\n    31→### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    32→- Status: **False** (proven to fail)\n    33→- Symbolic expression: The numerator of L₂ (after extracting the positive factor φ·(1+t)^(−199/50)·(1+x)^(−199/50) / 12500000) reduces to a polynomial in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² with coefficients depending on R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/25):\n    34→\n    35→  `f(z, R) = (14751 − 292R)·z² + (98 − 4R)·z + (14751 + 296R)`\n    36→\n    37→- Numerical sampling (engine default grid): min = 9.26×10⁻⁸ > 0 (likely_true on small grid)\n    38→- **However, extended numerical sampling found concrete counterexamples:**\n    39→  - t = 10⁵⁰, x = 0, c = 1: L₂ = −1.16×10⁻² < 0\n    40→  - Symmetric (1+t)=(1+x)=√(10⁵⁰), c = 2: L₂ = −1.50×10⁻¹⁰¹ < 0\n    41→  - t = 2.44×10⁴², x = 1, c = 1: L₂ = −5.03×10⁻⁶ < 0\n    42→\n    43→- Diagnosis: The z² coefficient of f(z, R) flips sign at R = 14751/292 ≈ 50.517, which corresponds to (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 3.85 × 10⁴². Beyond this threshold:\n    44→\n    45→  1. The quadratic in z becomes a downward-opening parabola\n    46→  2. Since the constant term (14751 + 296R) is always positive while the leading coefficient is negative, the quadratic has one negative root and one positive root z*(R)\n    47→  3. For z > z*(R), the quadratic (and hence L₂) is negative\n    48→  4. Since z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² is unbounded (c is a free positive parameter), z can always exceed z*(R) for any fixed R > 50.517\n    49→\n    50→  **Crucially**, even with c = 1 and the asymmetric case (t large, x = 0), z = (1+t)² = R⁵⁰ grows as R⁵⁰, which vastly exceeds z*(R) ∼ O(1) for large R. The dominant term for large R is −292·R¹⁰¹ → −∞.\n    51→\n    52→  The candidate log's numerical test (x, t ∈ [1, 10¹⁴]) was insufficient because (1+t)(1+x) ≤ (1+10¹⁴)² ≈ 10²⁸, giving R ≈ 10²⁸/²⁵ ≈ 10¹·¹² ≈ 13.2, well below R_flip ≈ 50.517. The failure region requires (1+t)(1+x) > 10⁴².⁶.\n    53→\n    54→  **This is the same structural failure as Round 2**, just pushed to larger scales. Changing λ from −1/20 to −1/50 moved R_flip from ≈ 20.5 to ≈ 50.5, but the fundamental issue persists: the R-dependent negative term in L₂ eventually dominates the constant positive term for any finite N in the family λ = −1/N.\n    55→\n    56→### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n    57→- Status: **True** (proven)\n    58→- Symbolic expression: Using u = 1/(1+t)², v = c²/(1+x)²:\n    59→\n    60→  `L₁² − L² − c²(φ_xt)² = (φ²/2500²) · (2400u² + 4803uv + 2400v²)`\n    61→\n    62→- Derivation:\n    63→  - L = (φ/2500)·(u − v), L₁ = (−49φ/2500)·(u + v)\n    64→  - L₁² − L² = (φ²/2500²)·[49²(u+v)² − (u−v)²] = (φ²/2500²)·(48u+50v)(50u+48v)\n    65→  - (48u+50v)(50u+48v) = 2400u² + 4804uv + 2400v²\n    66→  - φ_xt = φ/(2500·(1+t)·(1+x)), so c²φ_xt² = (φ²/2500²)·uv\n    67→  - Subtracting: 2400u² + 4804uv + 2400v² − uv = 2400u² + 4803uv + 2400v²\n    68→\n    69→- Numerical sampling: min = 3.46×10⁻⁸ > 0 (strictly positive everywhere)\n    70→- Diagnosis: The quadratic form Q(u,v) = 2400u² + 4803uv + 2400v² has all positive coefficients. Since u = 1/(1+t)² > 0 and v = c²/(1+x)² > 0 for all valid inputs, every term 2400u², 4803uv, 2400v² is strictly positive. Therefore Q(u,v) > 0 unconditionally on the domain {u > 0, v > 0}. This is a rigorous proof requiring no assumptions beyond the domain constraints.\n    71→\n    72→## Overall Summary\n    73→- Necessary condition 1: **True** (proven)\n    74→- Necessary condition 2: **True** (proven)\n    75→- Necessary condition 3: **False** (proven to fail at (1+t)(1+x) > 10^42.6)\n    76→- All necessary conditions: **False**\n    77→- Sufficient condition: **True** (proven)\n    78→- **ALL CONDITIONS PASS: False**\n    79→\n    80→## Failure Analysis\n    81→\n    82→### The L₂ ≥ 0 condition is the sole failure — same as Round 2\n    83→\n    84→The candidate passes conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition with clean, rigorous proofs. The only failure is L₂ ≥ 0 (condition 3), which fails when (1+t)(1+x) becomes astronomically large.\n    85→\n    86→### Why the parameter change λ: −1/20 → −1/50 didn't fix it\n    87→\n    88→The search agent's reasoning was correct that making |λ| smaller pushes the failure threshold to larger values:\n    89→- Round 2 (λ = −1/20): R_crit = 14751/292... wait, the coefficients change with N. For N = 20: R_crit ≈ 20.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10¹³·³\n    90→- Round 3 (λ = −1/50): R_crit ≈ 50.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10⁴²·⁶\n    91→\n    92→But the failure is **structural to the entire family** λ = −1/N with the double-log ansatz. Here is why:\n    93→\n    94→For λ = −1/N, the L₂ expression (after normalization) becomes a quadratic in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² with:\n    95→- z² coefficient: `(const_pos)·N⁴ − (const_pos)·N³·R` where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(2/N)\n    96→- The z² coefficient flips sign at R_crit ~ O(N)\n    97→- R_crit ~ N means (1+t)(1+x) ~ N^(N/2), which grows super-exponentially with N\n    98→- BUT: for any finite N, there exist t, x, c values making L₂ < 0\n    99→\n   100→The fix cannot come from making N larger within this family. The search agent should consider:\n   101→\n   102→1. **A fundamentally different ansatz for ψ** — the double-log ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x) has the perfect \"self-similar\" property for conditions 1 and the sufficient condition, but its L₂ structure inherently produces an R-dependent negative term that grows faster than the constant positive term.\n   103→\n   104→2. **Modifying the ansatz to suppress the R-dependent term in L₂** — the term involving φ³ · (tilde_square(ψ))² in L₂ generates the problematic −lam⁴·φ³·(tilde_box(ψ))² contribution. An ansatz where tilde_box(ψ) decays faster relative to the other L₂ terms might avoid this.\n   105→\n   106→3. **Exploring ansätze where L₂ is manifestly non-negative** — for instance, if ψ satisfies □ψ = 0 (wave equation) AND ψ̃ = 0, many of the problematic L₂ terms vanish. But this severely constrains ψ and may conflict with condition 2.\n   107→\n   108→4. **Considering non-separated ansätze** — the candidate log notes that the double-log is \"the unique separated ansatz with the self-similar property.\" Breaking the separability assumption might unlock new possibilities, though it would complicate all the other conditions.\n   109→\n   110→### Key mathematical insight for the next round\n   111→\n   112→The L₂ operator has a term −s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² which is always **negative** (since s = −1, s³ = −1, λ⁴ > 0, φ³ > 0, (ψ̃)² ≥ 0). This term must be dominated by the positive terms in L₂. For the double-log ansatz, this negative term grows as φ³ · (1/(1+t)² − c²/(1+x)²)², which involves φ³ = ((1+t)(1+x))^(3/N). The competing positive terms from the □(φ·□ψ) and □(φ·ψ̃) parts grow more slowly because they involve fewer powers of φ. For any finite N, the φ³ factor eventually wins.\n   113→\n   114→An ansatz that makes ψ̃ ≡ 0 (i.e., |ψ_t| = c|ψ_x|) would kill this problematic term entirely, but would need ψ to satisfy the eikonal equation, which may conflict with condition 2 requirements.\n   115→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d555f78-0034-4944-a628-75f8aad1227e","uuid":"7aebd460-dd9b-4765-99cc-32fbd1c3910a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_3/verification_report.md","content":"# Verification Report — Round 3\n\n## Candidate Summary\n- psi = -log(1 + t) - log(1 + x)\n- alpha = 1\n- s = -1\n- lambda = -1/50\n- Other params: none (same ansatz as Round 2, only λ changed from -1/20 to -1/50)\n\n## Condition Results\n\n### Necessary Condition 1: L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ\n- Status: **True** (proven)\n- Condition 1a (L − L₁ ≥ 0): **True** (proven)\n- Condition 1b (−L₁ − L ≥ 0): **True** (proven)\n- Symbolic expression (1a): `(24c²(1+t)² + 25(1+x)²) / (1250·(1+t)^(99/50)·(1+x)^(99/50))`\n- Symbolic expression (1b): `(25c²(1+t)² + 24(1+x)²) / (1250·(1+t)^(99/50)·(1+x)^(99/50))`\n- Numerical sampling: 1a min = 1.90×10⁻⁴, 1b min = 1.82×10⁻⁴ (both strictly positive)\n- Diagnosis: Both expressions are ratios with positive denominators. The numerators are sums of two manifestly positive terms (since c > 0, t ≥ 0, x ≥ 0, each squared term ≥ 1). Specifically:\n  - 1a numerator: 24c²(1+t)² ≥ 24c² > 0 and 25(1+x)² ≥ 25 > 0\n  - 1b numerator: 25c²(1+t)² ≥ 25c² > 0 and 24(1+x)² ≥ 24 > 0\n\n  This is a clean, rigorous proof. The key property enabling it is that with α = 1, the wave operator □ψ and the energy form ψ̃ coincide (since □ψ = ψ_tt − c²ψ_xx and ψ̃ = (ψ_t)² − c²(ψ_x)², and for this ansatz ψ_tt = (ψ_t)², ψ_xx = (ψ_x)²). This \"self-similar\" property of log functions makes L and L₁ have a particularly simple relationship.\n\n### Necessary Condition 2: lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n- Status: **True** (proven by SymPy)\n- λψ = (1/50)·(log(1+t) + log(1+x)) = (1/50)·log((1+t)(1+x))\n- Limit value: +∞\n- Diagnosis: Since λ = −1/50 and ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x), we get λψ = (1/50)·log((1+t)(1+x)). As x → +∞ with t fixed, this diverges to +∞. Trivially proven.\n\n### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n- Status: **False** (proven to fail)\n- Symbolic expression: The numerator of L₂ (after extracting the positive factor φ·(1+t)^(−199/50)·(1+x)^(−199/50) / 12500000) reduces to a polynomial in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² with coefficients depending on R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/25):\n\n  `f(z, R) = (14751 − 292R)·z² + (98 − 4R)·z + (14751 + 296R)`\n\n- Numerical sampling (engine default grid): min = 9.26×10⁻⁸ > 0 (likely_true on small grid)\n- **However, extended numerical sampling found concrete counterexamples:**\n  - t = 10⁵⁰, x = 0, c = 1: L₂ = −1.16×10⁻² < 0\n  - Symmetric (1+t)=(1+x)=√(10⁵⁰), c = 2: L₂ = −1.50×10⁻¹⁰¹ < 0\n  - t = 2.44×10⁴², x = 1, c = 1: L₂ = −5.03×10⁻⁶ < 0\n\n- Diagnosis: The z² coefficient of f(z, R) flips sign at R = 14751/292 ≈ 50.517, which corresponds to (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 3.85 × 10⁴². Beyond this threshold:\n\n  1. The quadratic in z becomes a downward-opening parabola\n  2. Since the constant term (14751 + 296R) is always positive while the leading coefficient is negative, the quadratic has one negative root and one positive root z*(R)\n  3. For z > z*(R), the quadratic (and hence L₂) is negative\n  4. Since z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² is unbounded (c is a free positive parameter), z can always exceed z*(R) for any fixed R > 50.517\n\n  **Crucially**, even with c = 1 and the asymmetric case (t large, x = 0), z = (1+t)² = R⁵⁰ grows as R⁵⁰, which vastly exceeds z*(R) ∼ O(1) for large R. The dominant term for large R is −292·R¹⁰¹ → −∞.\n\n  The candidate log's numerical test (x, t ∈ [1, 10¹⁴]) was insufficient because (1+t)(1+x) ≤ (1+10¹⁴)² ≈ 10²⁸, giving R ≈ 10²⁸/²⁵ ≈ 10¹·¹² ≈ 13.2, well below R_flip ≈ 50.517. The failure region requires (1+t)(1+x) > 10⁴².⁶.\n\n  **This is the same structural failure as Round 2**, just pushed to larger scales. Changing λ from −1/20 to −1/50 moved R_flip from ≈ 20.5 to ≈ 50.5, but the fundamental issue persists: the R-dependent negative term in L₂ eventually dominates the constant positive term for any finite N in the family λ = −1/N.\n\n### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n- Status: **True** (proven)\n- Symbolic expression: Using u = 1/(1+t)², v = c²/(1+x)²:\n\n  `L₁² − L² − c²(φ_xt)² = (φ²/2500²) · (2400u² + 4803uv + 2400v²)`\n\n- Derivation:\n  - L = (φ/2500)·(u − v), L₁ = (−49φ/2500)·(u + v)\n  - L₁² − L² = (φ²/2500²)·[49²(u+v)² − (u−v)²] = (φ²/2500²)·(48u+50v)(50u+48v)\n  - (48u+50v)(50u+48v) = 2400u² + 4804uv + 2400v²\n  - φ_xt = φ/(2500·(1+t)·(1+x)), so c²φ_xt² = (φ²/2500²)·uv\n  - Subtracting: 2400u² + 4804uv + 2400v² − uv = 2400u² + 4803uv + 2400v²\n\n- Numerical sampling: min = 3.46×10⁻⁸ > 0 (strictly positive everywhere)\n-